window.GNAMMA_SEQUENTIAL={"edition":"0.8","title":"al-Mustaṣfā — sequential technical English translation (working)","source":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","pages":{"V00P000":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"Praise belongs to God, the Strong and Powerful, the Protecting Friend and Helper, the Subtle and Overwhelming, the Avenger and Forgiver, the Hidden and Manifest, the First and Last: He who has made intellect the weightiest of treasures and stores, and knowledge the most profitable of acquisitions and trades, the noblest of attainments and distinctions, the most generous grounds of praise and the most praiseworthy of destinations. Through recording knowledge, pens and inkwells have been ennobled; through hearing it, prayer-niches and pulpits have been adorned; through inscribing it, leaves and books have been embellished; and through its distinction, the young have been set before their elders. By its brilliance inward secrets and consciences are illumined and hearts and inward sight enlightened. Beside it the light of the sun shining upon the turning firmament is accounted slight; in the face of its inner light even what appears as the light of the eyes is diminished, for its illumination penetrates the depths of the most obscure thoughts, though outward eyes grow weary, and veils and coverings grow thick.","note":"The rhetorical pairing of ʿaql and ʿilm is important for the later contrast between reasoning and mere transmission. The English preserves the cumulative panegyric rather than reducing it to a summary."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"May blessings be upon Muḥammad, His Messenger, of pure stock, resplendent nobility, surpassing honour and generous excellence, sent as a bearer of good tidings to believers and as a warner to unbelievers, whose revealed law supersedes every former law and passing religion. He was strengthened by the glorious Qurʾān, of which neither hearer nor transmitter grows weary; neither poet nor prose writer reaches the essence of its eloquence, and neither description nor recollection encompasses its marvels. Every person skilled in expression falls short of fully tasting the clarity of its secrets. May abundant blessings and peace be upon his family and companions, beyond what a human lifetime could count. To proceed: the judge of reason, a magistrate never dismissed or replaced, and the witness of revealed law, a witness whose probity is established and whose testimony accepted, together declare that this world is a place of delusion rather than lasting delight, a conveyance for action rather than idleness, a stopping place for passage rather than a garden of permanent enjoyment, a field of commerce rather than a settled residence. Its merchandise is obedience; its profit is success on the day when the Hour comes to pass.","note":"“Judge of reason” (qāḍī al-ʿaql) and “witness of revelation” (shāhid al-sharʿ) are intentional juridical metaphors and not two separate lawgivers."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"Obedience has two forms: action and knowledge. Of the two, knowledge is more conducive to success and brings the greater profit. For knowledge too is an action, but an action of the heart, the noblest of the bodily members, and an exertion of intellect, the most excellent of faculties; it is the vehicle of religious commitment and the bearer of the trust which, when offered to earth, mountains, and heaven, they feared to bear and refused to shoulder.","note":"This is an ethical ranking in the preface, not a claim that learning dispenses with practice; compare his later account of the sciences of the way to the hereafter."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The sciences, then, are of three kinds. The first is purely intellectual, such as arithmetic, geometry, astronomy, and analogous disciplines, toward which the revealed law neither urges nor specifically invites people. Some of these yield false conjectures that cling to the mind—indeed, some conjecture is sinful—while others yield true knowledge that brings no benefit. We seek refuge in God from knowledge that brings no benefit. And by “benefit” I do not mean present passions and sumptuous pleasures, for these perish and pass away; the benefit in question is recompense in the abode of the hereafter.","note":"The evaluative distinction must not be equated with a denial that arithmetic or geometry can yield demonstration; here al-Ghazālī is judging their religious benefit in this preface."}],"V01P003":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"A second kind is purely transmitted, such as prophetic reports and Qurʾānic commentaries. The task involved in such disciplines is comparatively slight, since young and old can stand on equal footing in mastering them: strength of memory suffices for transmission, which, as such, affords no exercise to reason. The noblest sciences, however, are those in which intellect and transmitted authority are joined, and considered judgment accompanies revealed law. Jurisprudence and its principles belong to this class. They draw a straight course from the choicest elements of both revelation and reason. They are neither an exercise of unaided intellect which revelation cannot accept, nor a structure built upon mere imitation without the confirming and correcting witness of reason.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Because of the excellence of jurisprudence and what gives it that excellence, God has made people eager to seek it; its scholars enjoy the highest rank and standing, and gather the greatest number of followers and supporters. In my early years, therefore, the distinctive benefits of this discipline for religion and worldly life, and the recompense of the next life and this, impelled me to devote the first portion of my allotted time to it and to reserve part of my remaining breath for its study. I composed many books on the detailed rulings of law and on its principles. Thereafter I turned to knowledge of the path to the afterlife and acquaintance with religion’s inward mysteries, and in that field composed extensive books, such as the Revival of the Religious Sciences, concise ones, such as The Jewels of the Qurʾān, and works of intermediate extent, such as The Alchemy of Happiness.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"God’s decree then brought me back to teaching and instruction. A group of advanced students of jurisprudence asked me to compose a treatise on legal theory in which I would unite systematic arrangement with rigorous examination, steering a middle course between a treatment too defective through omission and one too wearisome through excess. It was to be easier to grasp than The Refinement of Legal Principles, which inclines towards thoroughness and extensive treatment, but fuller than The Sifted Summary, which inclines towards brevity and condensation. I accepted their request with God’s assistance. I joined orderly arrangement to critical investigation, since the understanding of meanings cannot dispense with either. I therefore composed the work with an elegant and remarkable organization, so that at first sight the reader might grasp all the aims of the discipline and survey the entire field in which inquiry may proceed. Unless a student first gains command of a science’s principal divisions and foundations, there is no hope of reaching its deeper inquiries and ultimate goals. I have named it The Quintessence of the Science of Legal Principles [al-Mustaṣfā min ʿIlm al-Uṣūl]. It is God who is asked to grant success and guide us to the straight path; He is indeed the one who answers those who ask.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."}],"V01P004":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"Opening of the book. Know that the discipline called the principles of jurisprudence has been ordered and collected in this book and built upon a preliminary introduction and four axes. The introduction serves as a preparation and preface, while the axes contain the substance of what is intended. At the outset we shall discuss, first, the meaning of uṣūl al-fiqh, its definition, and its reality; second, its standing and relation to the sciences; third, how it branches into this introduction and the four axes; fourth, how all its divisions and details fall under these four axes; and fifth, how it is related to the introduction. On defining the principles of jurisprudence.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Understand that you cannot grasp the meaning of the principles of jurisprudence without first knowing what jurisprudence (fiqh) means. In its original linguistic sense, fiqh means knowledge and understanding: one says that a person understands good and evil, meaning that he knows and comprehends them. But in scholars’ established usage it has come to mean, specifically, knowledge of the revealed legal qualifications pertaining to the acts of those subject to the Law. Thus ordinary usage does not apply the name jurist (faqīh) to a theologian, philosopher, grammarian, scholar of transmitted reports, or exegete. It is reserved for those who know the legal qualifications of human acts—obligation, prohibition, permission, recommendation and reprehensibility; whether a contract is sound, defective, or void; whether an act of worship is performance within its time or discharge afterwards, and the like. You will also appreciate that acts have rationally ascertainable predicates, such as being accidents, inhering in a substrate, differing from substance, or constituting motion and rest. Someone who knows these is called a speculative theologian, not a jurist.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"It is the jurist who explains the legal qualifications of acts as obligatory, forbidden, permitted, reprehensible, or recommended. Once you have understood that, understand also that uṣūl al-fiqh is concerned with the proofs of these rulings and with knowing the ways in which those proofs indicate the rulings—considered in general, not in the detailed treatment of particular cases. The discipline of juristic disagreement (ʿilm al-khilāf), which itself belongs to jurisprudence, likewise deals with the proofs of rulings and their modes of indication, but in relation to individual questions: for example, what a particular report indicates in the dispute over marriage without a guardian, or what a particular verse indicates for the case of slaughter without pronouncing God’s name.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The principles, by contrast, do not take up an individual legal question as their subject, even in the manner of a worked example. Rather, their proper inquiry is into the foundation of the Book, the Sunna and consensus, the conditions of their authenticity and establishment, and then their general ways of indicating rulings: through linguistic form, through what the expression conveys by implication, through the course and requirements of the expression, or through the intelligible meaning discerned from it—that is, qiyās. They do not undertake the resolution of a particular legal question. This is what distinguishes the principles of jurisprudence from its branches.","note":"The source paragraph continues into the next digitized page; the English is divided at this precise boundary."}],"V01P005":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"You can therefore see that the evidences of rulings are the Book, the Sunna and consensus; the discipline concerned with how these three foundations are established, with the conditions of their soundness, and with the ways in which they indicate rulings is what is meant by uṣūl al-fiqh.","note":"Continuation of the Arabic paragraph beginning on V01P004 §3."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"On the place of this science and its relation to the other sciences.","note":"A source heading presented as its own reading unit."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"Understand that the sciences divide into rational disciplines, such as medicine, arithmetic and geometry—which are not our present concern—and religious disciplines, such as speculative theology (kalām), jurisprudence and its principles, the study of prophetic reports, Qurʾānic exegesis, and knowledge of the inward life: by the latter I mean knowledge of the heart and its purification from blameworthy character traits. Both the rational and the religious sciences can, in turn, be divided into comprehensive and particular disciplines.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"Within the religious sciences, kalām is the comprehensive discipline; the others—jurisprudence, its principles, the science of reports and exegesis—are particular sciences. For the exegete considers specifically the meaning of the Book; the traditionist the way in which a report is established; the jurist the legal qualifications of the acts of those subject to the Law; and the uṣūlī the proofs of revealed rulings. The speculative theologian, by contrast, considers what is most universal: being (al-mawjūd). He first divides what exists into eternal and originated; then originated being into substance and accident; then accidents into those requiring life—such as knowledge, will, power, speech, hearing and sight—and those not requiring it, such as colour, smell and taste. He divides substances into animals, plants and inanimate bodies, and examines whether their distinctions are differences of species or of accidents.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"The theologian then considers the Eternal and establishes that He is not multiple and cannot be divided as originated beings are divided, but must be one, distinguished from originated beings by attributes that necessarily belong to Him, properties impossible for Him, and states that are possible but neither necessary nor impossible in His regard. He thus distinguishes what is possible, necessary, and impossible with respect to Him.","note":"Wujūb, imkān and istiḥāla here are theological modalities; do not transpose them directly into the legal categories wājib/mubāḥ/ḥarām."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"He next shows that acting is in principle possible for God, and that the world is an act of His that was possible rather than necessary; precisely because it was possible, the world required one who brought it into being. Sending messengers is likewise among God's possible acts, and He possesses the power both to send them and to make their truthfulness known through miracles; this possible event has in fact taken place. Here the theologian's discourse reaches its end, and the independent jurisdiction of reason reaches its limit. For reason establishes the Prophet's truthfulness, but then steps aside, acknowledging that what the Prophet says about God, the Last Day, and other matters which reason cannot discover on its own—and which it also does not judge impossible—must be accepted. Revelation can indeed bring what unaided reason cannot independently apprehend. Reason cannot on its own know that obedience brings felicity in the next life or disobedience misery; but neither does it judge such a relation impossible. And reason judges that someone whose truthfulness has been attested by a miracle must speak truly. When he then reports these matters, reason assents to them on that basis. This, then, is the scope of kalām. You can see that the theologian begins with the most universal object, namely being (al-mawjūd), and gradually descends to the particulars just mentioned, thereby establishing the principles on which the other religious sciences rest: the Book, the Sunna, and the Messenger's truthfulness. The exegete selects one particular matter examined by the theologian—the Book—and undertakes its interpretation. The traditionist selects another—the Sunna—and investigates how it is established. The jurist takes the act of the legally accountable person and examines its relation to the revealed legal address, in terms of obligation, prohibition, or permission. And the legal theorist takes one particular matter, the utterance of the Messenger, whom the theologian has established to be truthful, and investigates the way in which that utterance indicates legal rulings: by its explicit wording, its implied meaning, or its intelligible import and what may be inferred from it. The legal theorist's proper inquiry does not go beyond the Prophet's words and acts.","note":"“Steps aside” translates the striking “then dismisses itself” metaphor: the theologian establishes prophetic veracity, while the uṣūlī can presuppose this rather than reproving it. The last clause continues on the next page."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"For the Book…","note":"Original sentence crosses the digital page break and continues at V01P006 §0."}],"V01P006":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…is heard only through his utterance, and consensus is established by his utterance. Thus the proofs consist only of the Book, the Sunna, and consensus. The truthfulness of the Messenger's speech, however, and its authoritative evidentiary status, are established in kalām.","note":"This completes the original Arabic sentence spanning V01P005–V01P006. The source word “only” pertains to this specific architectonic argument, not an independent debate about every possible source category."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Kalām, then, undertakes to establish the first principles of all the religious sciences, which are particular in relation to it. In this respect kalām ranks as the highest of the sciences, since one descends from it to these particular disciplines. An objection may be raised: Should it not then be a condition of being an uṣūlī, jurist, exegete or traditionist that one have mastered kalām? How could someone descend to a lower, particular science without first completing the higher, comprehensive one? We reply: this is not a condition for being an uṣūlī, jurist, exegete or traditionist, though it is a condition for being a scholar in the unrestricted sense, fully equipped with the religious sciences. Every particular science has first principles accepted as given within it, whose demonstration is sought in some other science.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"The jurist investigates the relation of a legally accountable person’s act to the Law’s address, commanding or forbidding it. He is not required to demonstrate that human beings perform voluntary acts—although the determinists have denied human agency, while another group has denied accidents, and an act is an accident. Nor is the jurist obliged to prove the existence of a revealed legal address or the doctrine that God possesses speech subsisting in Him which commands and forbids. Instead, he takes as granted the existence of God’s address and the act of the accountable person, and studies the relation between them. In doing so he has reached the proper terminus of his discipline. Likewise, the uṣūlī accepts from the theologian, without himself demonstrating it there, that the Messenger’s utterance is authoritative proof whose truth must be acknowledged; he then investigates its modes of indication and the conditions of its authenticity.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"Every specialist in a particular science is therefore necessarily an acceptor-on-authority (muqallid) with respect to his science's premises, until he ascends to the higher science that establishes them. At that point, however, he has passed beyond his own discipline into another.","note":"Taqlīd here denotes accepting principles established in another discipline, not the lay follower’s reliance on a mujtahid in matters of law. The same term serves distinguishable functions."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"On how the science turns upon its four axes. Once you understand that the uṣūlī investigates how transmitted proofs indicate legal rulings, you will see that the object is to know how to draw rulings from their proofs. We must therefore consider, first, the rulings themselves; second, the proofs and their classes; third, how rulings are drawn from proofs; and fourth, the qualities of the one entitled to draw them. For rulings are fruits, and every fruit has a character and nature of its own, a source that produces it, someone who harvests it, and a manner of harvesting.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"The fruits are the rulings: obligation, prohibition, recommendation, reprehensibility, permission, the good and the bad, compensatory performance and timely performance, legal soundness and defectiveness, and other such qualifications. The productive sources are the proofs, and these are three only: the Book, the Sunna and consensus. The ways of harvesting are the modes by which proofs indicate their conclusions, and these are four: utterances indicate something either through their linguistic form and expressed wording; through what their sense and implication convey; through what they necessarily require or presuppose; or through the intelligible meaning inferred from them.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"The harvester is the independent jurist (mujtahid), whose qualities, conditions and legal status must be known. The whole of legal theory therefore turns around four principal axes:","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":7,"en":"The first axis concerns rulings. It is fitting to begin with them, for they are the fruit sought.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":8,"en":"The second axis concerns the proofs—the Book, the Sunna, and consensus. They rightly come next, since once the fruit is known nothing is more important than knowing what produces it.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":9,"en":"The third axis concerns the method of harvesting: the ways in which proofs indicate rulings. These are four: indication by what is expressly formulated, by implication, by necessary presupposition and entailment, and by an intelligible meaning discerned from the wording.","note":"Previously translated and now page-aligned to the full OpenITI witness."},{"sourceParagraph":10,"en":"The fourth axis concerns the one who gathers the fruit, the mujtahid who judges according to his preponderant estimation (ẓann), and, corresponding to him, the follower (muqallid)…","note":"The Arabic source sentence continues on V01P007 §0, where it states the follower’s relation to the jurist."}],"V01P007":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"whom he must follow. We must therefore describe the conditions and qualifications of the follower (muqallid) and of the independent jurist (mujtahid).","note":"Continuation from the preceding OpenITI page, which includes the beginning of the sentence."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"You may ask: “The discipline of uṣūl al-fiqh comprises many chapters and scattered subdivisions. How can they all be brought under these four axes?” We answer: the first axis is the legal ruling (al-ḥukm).","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"A legal ruling has its own reality and divisions. It has a relation to the one who makes the ruling, namely, the Lawgiver; to the one upon whom it is imposed, namely, the legally accountable person; to that concerning which the ruling is made, namely, the legally accountable person's act; and to that by which the ruling is made manifest, namely, an occasioning factor (sabab) and an operative legal ground (ʿilla). Investigation of the reality of the ruling will establish that it is an address of revealed law (khiṭāb al-sharʿ), not a feature belonging to the act, nor its goodness or badness; reason does not itself constitute the ruling, and there is no revealed-law ruling before the arrival of revelation. Investigation of its classes will clarify what obligation, prohibition, recommendation, permission, reprehensibility, compensatory and timely performance, validity and invalidity, strict rule and concession mean, together with the other kinds of ruling. Investigation of the ruler will establish that rule-making authority belongs to God alone: it does not belong independently to the Messenger, or to a master over a servant, or to one created being over another. Each such legal relation rests upon God's ruling and institution, not the other's autonomous authority. Investigation of the addressee will address the person who forgets, the coerced person and the child; the applicability of subsidiary legal demands to the unbeliever; the intoxicated person; and those who can or cannot properly be charged with duties. Investigation of the subject of a ruling will show that the address concerns acts, not concrete objects in themselves, and that it is not an intrinsic feature of those acts. Investigation of what discloses the ruling will explain the occasioning factor, operative ground, condition, locus, and sign. This first axis thus gathers many scattered discussions that earlier writers distributed in unrelated locations, without any common organizing relation; consequently students could not discern their purposes, why they needed to study them, or how they belonged to legal theory. The second axis concerns the producing sources: the Book, the Sunna, and consensus. Investigation of the Book will clarify what constitutes the Qurʾān and what does not, and how it is established—namely, by recurrent mass transmission (tawātur) alone—as well as what it can contain in the way of literal and figurative expression, Arabic and non-Arabic wording. Investigation of the Sunna will examine the Messenger's utterances and acts; their establishment by mass-transmitted or individual reports; their forms of transmission, whether connected or transmitted with a missing link; and the qualities of transmitters, including uprightness and grounds for rejection, until the entire discussion of reports is complete. Connected with the Book and Sunna is the treatment of abrogation, for abrogation concerns these two alone: consensus itself cannot be abrogated. Investigation of consensus will establish its nature, authority, divisions, the consensus of the Companions and of succeeding generations, and the whole range of associated questions. The third axis concerns the methods by which the jurist draws out the fruits.","note":"The two conditions sabab and ʿilla are deliberately differentiated from efficient causality; Arabic uses al-muẓhir lahu, the manifestation of the ruling. “No ruling before revelation” is stated as an Ashʿarī thesis concerning the specific legal address, not the denial of factual natural truths."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"These methods are four. First is indication by the linguistic form of an utterance; this concerns the imperative and prohibitive forms, general and particular expressions, apparent wording, interpreted wording, and explicit text. The books on commands and prohibitions and on general and particular terms examine what these linguistic forms require. Indication through what the words convey by sense and implication is treated in the discussions of implied meaning (mafhūm) and the argument from the address (dalīl al-khiṭāb).","note":"The last Arabic words “wa-dalīl” continue into “al-khiṭāb” at the top of the next digital page. The English supplies the continuation for readability."}],"V01P008":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"As for indication by what the utterance necessarily presupposes and requires, it includes implications such as this: someone says, “Emancipate your slave on my behalf,” and the addressee replies, “I have emancipated him.” Their words presuppose that the requesting party has acquired ownership, although neither expressed this explicitly. Such ownership is necessitated and required by what they have actually said. Indication through the intelligible meaning of an utterance may be illustrated by the Prophet's saying, “A judge must not adjudicate while angry”: through the discernible basis of its meaning it also extends to a judge suffering hunger, illness, or the pressing need to relieve himself. Juridical qiyās arises from this mode of indication, and this leads to the exposition of all the rulings and subdivisions of qiyās. The fourth axis concerns the person who gathers the fruit, the independent jurist, opposed to the follower. It treats their respective qualifications, the sphere in which independent inquiry is available and that in which it is not, whether independent jurists are correct in their conclusions, and the other rules governing ijtihād. This is the range of matters contained in legal theory, and you have now seen how its questions branch out from the four axes.","note":"Dalālat al-iqtiḍāʾ concerns a presupposition required for intelligible legal effect, and must not be rendered by the looser “suggestion.” The extension from anger to other impairments belongs to maʿqūl al-lafẓ and should not be identified with purely lexical denotation."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Understand that, when uṣūl al-fiqh was defined as knowledge of the proofs of rulings, its definition contained three expressions: knowledge (maʿrifa), proof (dalīl), and ruling (ḥukm). People therefore said: just as the nature of a ruling must be known, to the extent that the study of rulings constitutes one of the four axes, one must know what a proof is and know what knowledge itself is, namely, ʿilm. The kind of knowledge sought, moreover, can be reached only through disciplined inquiry (naẓar), so the nature of such inquiry must also be understood. Accordingly, they began to define knowledge, proof, and inquiry. But they did not stop at setting out the concepts of these matters; their discussion led them to argue for the existence of knowledge against the sophists who deny it, and for the validity of investigation against those who deny investigation. They then treated many divisions of sciences and proofs. This exceeds the bounds of the present discipline and mixes it with speculative theology (kalām). The theologians among the legal theorists treated these matters expansively because theology prevailed in their intellectual habits; affection for their own craft induced them to mingle it with this one. In the same manner, affection for language and grammar led some legal theorists to introduce grammatical discussions, including extended treatments of the meanings of particles and of inflection that properly belong to grammar. And affection for jurisprudence led some Transoxanian jurists, such as Abū Zayd and his followers, to intermingle numerous detailed questions of positive law with uṣūl: although they offered them as examples illustrating the operation of a principle in a particular case, they treated them excessively. The theologians have a more plausible excuse for including definitions of knowledge, inquiry, and proof in legal theory than they do for offering demonstrations of their existence against those who deny them. A definition establishes the conceptual forms of these things in the mind, and one must at least conceive them if one intends to discuss them—just as someone engaged in jurisprudence must at least conceive what consensus and qiyās are.","note":"The objection is disciplinary rather than anti-logical: conception of an object and a proof of its existence belong to different tasks. Al-Ghazālī is drawing a line between what legal theory presupposes and what it must establish."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"By contrast, establishing the authoritative probative force (ḥujjiyya) of consensus and qiyās is distinctive to legal theory itself. Arguing against those who deny the probative force of knowledge and of inquiry is to drag matters extraneous to uṣūl into it. Nevertheless, now that we have explained the excesses involved in such admixture, we will not omit all of it from this book. Weaning people from what they are accustomed to is difficult, and the mind recoils from what is unfamiliar. We will therefore confine ourselves to what is evidently useful to the sciences in general: explaining the intellect's means of apprehension and its progression from immediately necessary cognitions (ḍarūriyyāt) to the conclusions of theoretical investigation (naẓariyyāt), so that the nature of knowledge, inquiry, and proof, their divisions and their warrants, may be set out with a precision not found in the ordinary books of kalām.","note":"The logical prolegomenon is thus presented as generally useful but extrinsic to the special definition of legal theory."}],"V01P009":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"Introduction to the Book: the restriction of the means by which theoretical knowledge is acquired to definition and demonstration. In this introduction we shall explain the intellect's means of apprehension and how they are exhausted by definition (ḥadd) and demonstration (burhān). We shall state the conditions and kinds of a genuine definition and of a genuine demonstration, following a shorter treatment than we gave in The Touchstone of Inquiry (Miḥakk al-Naẓar) and The Standard of Knowledge (Miʿyār al-ʿIlm). This introduction is neither part of legal theory itself nor a preliminary peculiar to it: rather, it is an introduction to every science. Anyone without command of it has no secure basis for confidence in his knowledge. Readers who wish to omit this introduction may begin the book with the first axis, for there the proper subject matter of uṣūl al-fiqh begins. All theoretical sciences stand in the same need of this introduction as legal theory does.","note":"Explicit primary-text cross-reference to Miḥakk al-Naẓar and Miʿyār al-ʿIlm, not an inferred thematic similarity. “Demonstration” translates burhān here; not every later juristic proof meets this epistemic standard."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"On the restriction of the means by which theoretical knowledge is acquired to definition and demonstration: understand that apprehending things takes two forms. The first is apprehending individual concepts: knowing, for example, what “body,” “motion,” “world,” “originated,” and “eternal” mean, together with everything indicated by single names. The second is apprehending a relation between such concepts, whether by denial or affirmation. Thus one first knows the meaning of “world,” a single concept, and of “originated” and “eternal,” likewise individual concepts; then one relates a concept to another by negation or affirmation. One denies eternity of the world by saying, “The world is not eternal,” or affirms origination of it by saying, “The world is originated.” Only this second form admits of affirmation as true or rejection as false.","note":"“Origination” (ḥudūth) is a technical cosmological predicate. Rendering ḥādith simply as “recent” would erase the distinction between the world and the eternal."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"The first form, on the other hand, cannot be true or false. Truth and falsity pertain only to a declarative statement, which must consist of at least two individual elements, something described and a description. Once the predicate is connected to the subject through affirmation or denial, the resulting statement may be true or false. But an isolated utterance such as “originated,” “body,” or “eternal” is neither true nor false. It is unobjectionable to adopt distinct expressions for these two sorts of apprehension, since things differing in kind should have correspondingly different names: words serve as counterparts to meanings and should be aligned with them.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The logicians call the apprehension of individual concepts conception (taṣawwur), and apprehension of the assertoric relation between them assent (taṣdīq). Thus they say that cognition is either conception or assent. Some of our scholars call the first recognition (maʿrifa) and the second knowledge (ʿilm), following the grammarians' observation that the verb “to recognize” takes one object, as when one says, “I recognized Zayd,” while the verb “to suppose” takes two, as in, “I supposed Zayd to be learned.” One does not say merely, “I supposed Zayd,” or “I supposed learned.” “To know” behaves grammatically like “to suppose”: one says, “I knew Zayd to be just.”","note":"The distinction is logical rather than a claim that only taṣdīq is knowledge in all accepted Arabic usage. Al-Ghazālī explicitly acknowledges divergent terminologies."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"Usage differs across these technical vocabularies. Once you understand the distinction between the two kinds, there is no need to quarrel over names. We now say that…","note":"The clause begins an argument continued at the start of V01P010; the ellipsis marks a genuine page break, not omitted argumentative content."}],"V01P010":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…apprehensions are exhausted by recognition and knowledge, or by conception and assent. Every item of knowledge involving assent necessarily presupposes two recognitions, that is, two conceptions: someone who does not know the individual concepts cannot know what is composed from them. How could one know that the world is originated without understanding what “world” and “originated” mean? Recognition of single concepts is itself of two kinds. The first is primary, and needs no investigative search: its meaning is impressed upon the mind without inquiry, as with “existence,” “thing,” and many objects of sense experience. The second is sought: its name indicates something only generally, without expounding or distinguishing its meaning, so one seeks its explanation by a definition. Assent likewise divides into primary knowledge, such as necessary truths, and sought knowledge, such as the conclusions reached by theoretical inquiry.","note":"“Primary” (awwalī) in this classification is not interchangeable with “revealed”; “necessary truths” (ḍarūriyyāt) are an epistemological category."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"The sought object of recognition can be grasped only through definition, while a sought item of knowledge open to affirmation or denial can be grasped only through demonstration. Definition and demonstration are therefore the instruments by which all other sought knowledge is acquired. This introduction on the intellect's modes of apprehension will accordingly have two supports: one devoted to definition and one to demonstration.","note":"The categorical “only” states the book’s logical programme. It should be read alongside al-Ghazālī’s qualification that the forms of premises affect the certainty of conclusions."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"First Support: Definition. First Division: Rules. This support must come first because single concepts precede knowledge of composites. It contains two divisions: one comprising the rules, the other serving as tests or examinations of those rules.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The first division deals with the rules, which are six. The first rule is as follows.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"A definition is given in conversation in answer to a question, but not as the answer to every kind of question—only certain kinds. A question is a request and necessarily has an object sought as well as a form of expression. Requests and formulations are numerous, but the principal things sought are four.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"The first kind is sought with the expression “Is it…?” (hal). Two things may be requested by it: either existence itself, as in the question “Does God exist?”; or a state or attribute of what exists, as in “Is God the creator of human beings?” and “Does God speak, command, and forbid?”","note":"Hal is a technical logical interrogative; “whether” sometimes expresses its function more accurately than the standalone English “is.”"},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"The second kind is sought with “What…?” (mā).","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":7,"en":"This expression can be used to request three different things.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":8,"en":"First, it can request an explanation of a word. Someone who does not know the term ʿaqār, for example, asks “What is ʿaqār?” and receives the answer “Wine,” if the word “wine” is familiar to him.","note":"The example deliberately distinguishes synonymic explanation from explanation of the thing itself; the Arabic ʿaqār is a name used for wine."},{"sourceParagraph":9,"en":"Second, it can request an exact expression that includes everything intended and excludes everything else, distinguishing the thing asked about from other things however the distinction is accomplished. The expression might mention properties and necessary concomitants remote from the object's essence, or it might state the essence itself; the difference between essential and incidental characteristics will be explained below. Thus, if asked “What is wine?”, one might answer: “The liquid that throws up foam, then turns sour, and is kept in a cask.” One is not trying here to grasp wine's essential nature, but to collect enough of its incidental and concomitant characteristics that together they apply exactly to wine, neither leaving out a kind of wine nor including something that is not wine.","note":"“Inclusive and exclusive” translates jāmiʿ māniʿ, the traditional conditions of an adequate distinguishing formulation. This is not yet a real definition (ḥadd ḥaqīqī)."},{"sourceParagraph":10,"en":"Third, it can request the quiddity (māhiyya) and essential reality of a thing. Thus, if one asks “What is wine?” and receives the reply “An intoxicating drink extracted from grapes,” the reply discloses its essential nature, and discrimination from other things necessarily follows. In ordinary usage the term “definition” is applied to these three forms without distinguishing them. We should therefore assign a separate name to each.","note":"Quiddity is “whatness”—the answer sought by the logical “What is it?”—and is not automatically the same as the legal operative ground of a ruling."}],"V01P011":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"Let us call the first a nominal definition (ḥadd lafẓī), since the questioner asks only for an explanation of the word; the second a descriptive definition (ḥadd rasmī), since what is sought is a concept impressed upon the mind which distinguishes its object without attaining its essential reality; and the third an essential or real definition (ḥadd ḥaqīqī), since the questioner seeks to grasp the thing's reality. The third must contain all the essential constituents of the thing. For example, if one is asked to define an animal and replies “a sentient body,” one has given an essential feature, and perhaps a description sufficient to include the intended things and exclude the others; nevertheless it is incomplete. One must add “capable of motion by choice,” for the essential reality of an animal is grasped by the intellect through the conjunction of these two features. The person who wishes merely to distinguish the object may be content with “sentient,” without even specifying “body.”","note":"The term rasmī belongs to the logical taxonomy here. Translating all three forms simply as “definition” loses Ghazālī’s distinction between synonym, discrimination by properties and statement of essence."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"The third kind of inquiry is expressed by “Why…?” (lima). It asks after an explanatory cause (ʿilla), and the response comes through demonstration, the nature of which will be set out below.","note":"The ʿilla sought in this logical question is explanatory, and must not automatically be conflated with the legally operative attribute in qiyās."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"The fourth kind is expressed by “Which…?” (ayy). It seeks to distinguish something whose general class is already known from other things with which it has been confused. For example, if one asks “What is a tree?” and is told “It is a body,” one should continue, “Which sort of body is it?”; the answer is “A growing one.” Questions beginning “How?”, “Where?”, “When?”, and the other interrogative forms fall under the request made with “Is it…?”; they seek an attribute or condition of an existent.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The second rule: whoever formulates a definition must be clear-sighted about the distinction among essential attributes, necessary concomitants, and incidental attributes. This distinction is subtle and requires explanation. When one meaning is related to a thing capable of receiving it as an attribute, it will be found, in relation to that subject, either to be essential to it, in which case it is called an attribute belonging to its very self; or to be inseparable, in which case it is called something following upon it; or to be incidental, in which case separation from it in existence is conceivable. These relations must be mastered, because they are useful alike in definition and in demonstration.","note":"The threefold distinction here is between dhātī, lāzim and ʿāriḍ, not between legal obligation and permissibility."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"By “essential” I mean whatever enters into the quiddity and essential reality of a thing in such a way that its meaning cannot be understood without understanding that constituent. Thus being coloured enters into the conception of blackness; and corporeality into the conception of horse and tree. A person who grasps “tree” has grasped a particular kind of body. Corporeality therefore enters into what it is to be a tree, an entry by which its reality is constituted in existence and in thought. If corporeality were removed, the existence of a tree as a tree would cease; if it were removed from thought, the understanding of tree and horse would vanish from the mind. Every feature of this kind must be included in the thing's definition. Anyone defining a plant must therefore say that it is “a growing body.”","note":"The examples presuppose a particular logical account of genus and constitutive difference; the definitions should not be silently modernized into biological taxonomy."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"An inseparable concomitant (lāzim), by contrast, never parts from the thing, yet understanding the thing's essential nature and quiddity does not depend upon it. A horse, plant, or tree casts a shadow when the sun rises. Those who describe regularly occurring processes in terms of necessary concomitance may regard the shadow as incapable of failing to occur under those conditions. Nevertheless, it is something consequent upon the thing, not a constituent of its essence. By this I mean that understanding what the thing is does not depend upon conceiving its shadow: someone oblivious to shadows still understands what a horse or plant is, and indeed what a body is, although the shadow never comes to mind. Similarly, “being created” is an inseparable attribute of the earth; it cannot be parted from it. Yet grasping the meaning of “earth” does not depend on knowing that it was created. Someone may understand what earth and heaven are before knowing that they were created. We first grasp the reality of a body and then seek to prove by demonstration that it is originated; but we cannot grasp earth and heaven without conceiving body.","note":"Ghazālī explicitly distinguishes the intelligibility of a quiddity from establishing its createdness. The existence of shadows and the necessity attaching to them are qualified by the habits-of-nature discussion; do not present this as an independent endorsement of physical necessitarianism."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"As for an incidental attribute (ʿāriḍ), I mean one whose continuing attachment is not necessary, and separation from which is conceivable: it may be something which departs…","note":"The source paragraph is divided by the OpenITI page boundary and continues at V01P012 §0."}],"V01P012":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…quickly, like the flush of embarrassment, or only slowly, like the yellow of gold, the blue of an eye, or the blackness of a Zanj (the historical ethnonym used here). Such an attribute may never in fact disappear, as with the blue of an eye, yet one can imagine it being absent. That the earth is created, on the other hand, and that an opaque body casts a shadow by obstructing sunlight, are inseparable features whose absence cannot be conceived in the relevant circumstances.","note":"The original uses the historical term al-Zanj. It is retained as an ethnonym and not endorsed as modern human classification; the philosophical point is whether an attribute can be conceived as absent."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Many fallacies arise from confusing what necessarily follows upon a thing with what belongs to its essence, for they share the feature of being inseparable. Exhaustively investigating that confusion would be impossible in an introduction that is merely supplementary to the present discipline; we have dealt with it extensively in The Standard of Knowledge (Miʿyār al-ʿIlm).","note":"Another explicit primary-text reference to Miʿyār al-ʿIlm. It supports a direct cross-work link."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"Once you understand the distinction between an essential constituent and an inseparable concomitant, include in a real definition only essential constituents, and include all of them, so that the entirety of the thing's reality and quiddity can be conceived. By “quiddity” I mean what is properly said in answer to “What is it?” A person who asks this seeks the reality of the thing; hence the answer contains only what is essential. Essential constituents divide into the general, called genus (jins), and the specific, called species (nawʿ). When the most general essential constituent has no more general genus above it, it is called the highest genus; when the most specific constituent has no narrower species beneath it, it is called the lowest species. This is the logicians' terminology, and we may agree to employ it without harm, for something like it is in use in our own disciplines too. For example, we say that substance (jawhar) is divided into body and what is not a body; body into what grows and what does not; what grows into animal and non-animal; and animal into rational—the human being—and non-rational. On this ordering, substance is the highest genus, for nothing more general stands above it as a genus; the human being is the lowest species, for no narrower species stands beneath it. Something that grows is a species relative to body, because it is more specific, but a genus relative to animal, because it is more general. Animal similarly stands between the more general class of growing things and the more specific human species.","note":"Terms jins/nawʿ are logical genus and species, not the modern Linnaean biological ranks; jawhar is “substance” in philosophical classification, not automatically “atom.”"},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"If someone asks: “How can nothing be more general than substance, when ‘being existent’ is more general? And how can nothing be more specific than human being, when ‘old,’ ‘young,’ ‘tall,’ ‘short,’ ‘writer,’ and ‘tailor’ are more specific?” we answer: by genus we do not mean just any more general predicate. We mean a more general predicate essential to the thing, one that belongs within the answer to “What is it?” such that removing assent to its being present from the mind would abolish the thing defined and its essential reality from the mind, so that it would no longer be an intelligible object. In this terminology “existent” does not enter into quiddity, for denying its existence need not remove the conception of its essence from the mind.","note":"Important for the wider project: the distinction between essence and existence is a claim within Ghazālī’s logical account and should not automatically be mapped onto Thomistic esse/essentia or Heideggerian being."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"For illustration, if someone asks “What is the definition of a triangle?” and we answer “A figure bounded by three sides,” or asks about a heptagon and we answer “A figure bounded by seven sides,” the questioner understands the definition of a heptagon without knowing whether any such figure actually exists in the world. Not knowing that it exists does not erase the grasp of its quiddity from the mind. But remove the conception of figure, and the conception of heptagon itself disappears. In the same way, being tall or short, old or young, a writer, white, or a practitioner of a trade does not enter into the human essence, for varying any of these does not change the answer to the question of quiddity. If we point to someone and ask “What is this?” and answer “A human being,” then he grows from childhood into adulthood or from short stature into tall stature, and we ask again “What is it?”—not “Who is he?”—the answer remains exactly the same.","note":"The triangle/heptagon example establishes conceptual independence of quiddity from the claim of actual existence, not the physical construction of a heptagon."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"If, however, one pointed to what is emitted from the reproductive organ in sexual intercourse and asked “What is it?”, the answer would be “A drop of semen.” When it becomes an embryo and then a newborn and one asks again “What is it?”, the answer changes: it would no longer be appropriate to call it a drop, but one would say “A human being.” Similarly, when water is heated and one asks what it is, the reply is “Water,” just as when it was cold. But if fire transforms it into vapour and then into air, the answer changes. Since attributes thus divide into those whose alteration changes the answer to the question of quiddity and those whose alteration does not, a real definition should mention those constituents which enter into the quiddity.","note":"These are medieval illustrative physical and embryological examples; they are not empirical commitments of the new editor."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"As for nominal and descriptive definitions…","note":"The clause continues on the next OpenITI page; no substantive conclusion is supplied where the witness continues."}],"V01P013":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…are comparatively easy to meet, because the person who seeks them is satisfied when one substitutes “wine” for the expression ʿaqār, or “recognition” for “knowledge,” or gives a set of distinguishing and mutually exclusive incidental features. What is genuinely difficult, indeed often unattainable, is the real definition: the one that discloses the quiddity of the thing and nothing else.","note":"Continuation of a sentence beginning with the nominal and descriptive definitions at the end of V01P012."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"The third rule: whenever you are asked what a thing is and wish to define it essentially, there are duties that must be fulfilled for the definition to be genuine. Neglect them, and we shall call the result descriptive or nominal instead, for it will no longer convey the thing's reality or represent its meaning adequately in the mind.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"First, assemble the constituents of a definition from genus and differentiae. If someone points to what grows out of the ground and asks “What is it?”, you must reply “A body.” But that by itself includes a stone, so you need to add “growing,” which excludes whatever does not grow. This exclusion is called a differentia (faṣl), because it separates the thing defined from other things.","note":"Faṣl here is a constitutive differentia in the logic of definition, not a chapter division."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"Second, mention every essential constituent, even were there a thousand of them, without shrinking from a lengthy expression. But the more general must precede the more specific: do not place the differentia before the genus as in “growing, body”; rather say “body, growing” (jism nāmin).","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"If you neglected that order, the expression would become confused, though the essential nature would still have been stated despite its awkward wording. Such a fault is less serious than the first, where you stopped at “body” alone.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"Third, when the proximate genus is available, do not also include a remote genus, thereby becoming repetitive—as if one said “a liquid beverage”—nor content yourself with the remote genus, thus moving too far away. For example, if you defined wine as “an intoxicating body derived from grapes,” you would have given something essential that is both coextensive and exclusive; but the definition would remain defective and inadequate to represent the full reality of wine. “Intoxicating liquid” is closer than “intoxicating body,” but still weak. You should instead say “intoxicating beverage,” since “beverage” is the nearer and more specific genus, beyond which no more specific genus is found. After naming the genus, search for the differentia: “beverage” includes many kinds of drink, so seek an essential constituent that will distinguish this one. If that proves difficult—and in most definitions it does—then after giving the genus turn to its necessary concomitants. Try to choose ones that are manifest and well known, for an obscure feature will not make the object known.","note":"The passage explicitly recognizes that exact essential definitions are often unattainable; “descriptive definition” is not merely a careless error but an ordinary fallback when essences are difficult to disclose."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"Thus if asked “What is a lion?” and you reply “A beast with foul breath,” intending to distinguish it from a dog by this trait, your distinguishing property is obscure, even if peculiar to the lion. “A brave beast with broad shoulders,” by contrast, uses features and concomitants more suited to the purpose because they are clearer.","note":"The example reflects traditional natural-historical assumptions; its point is logical clarity, not a modern zoological description."},{"sourceParagraph":7,"en":"Most of the definitions encountered in books are therefore descriptive, for grasping essential nature is exceedingly difficult. One may readily grasp some essential features but struggle with others; to grasp all such features without omitting a single one is difficult. So is distinguishing essential from concomitant features, preserving the order from general to particular, and finding the proximate genus. In defining a lion, for example, one might say “a brave animal” because the word “beast” does not come to mind, thereby accumulating several difficulties. The best descriptive definitions state the proximate genus and complete it by prominent and familiar distinguishing properties.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":8,"en":"Fourth, avoid unfamiliar and harsh expressions, far-fetched metaphors, and equivocal words. Be as concise as you can, and seek an unambiguous expression wherever possible. If no explicit expression is available and a metaphor must be used, choose the metaphor closest to the intended purpose and explain your meaning to the questioner. Not everything the intellect can conceive has been assigned an explicit expression to convey it.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":9,"en":"If someone uses many words, chooses a metaphor, or employs an ambiguous expression but makes his intention clear—either explicitly or through context—his conduct should not be regarded as a grave fault or be met with excessive…","note":"Page break mid-sentence; continuing at V01P014 §0."}],"V01P014":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…censure, provided he has disclosed the essential reality by stating all its essential constituents. That is the object sought; concision and other refinements are improvements and ornaments, like spices added to the food that is the real aim. Only the pedantic treat these matters as enormously important and condemn them vehemently, because their limited dispositions incline toward accessories, conventional forms, and subordinate details rather than the primary purpose. They may even reject someone who defines knowledge as “secure confidence in what is known” or “apprehension of what is known,” objecting that “confidence” can mean either trustworthiness or understanding. This is foolish: when confidence is linked to a known object, its sense as understanding becomes determinate.","note":"The critique is aimed at mistaking terminological fastidiousness for conceptual adequacy."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Likewise, if one defines colour as “that which is perceived by the faculty of sight in such and such a way,” one should not object that the word ʿayn can mean the pan of a balance, the sun, or the organ of sight. The word “faculty” or “sense” removes the ambiguity, so the necessary understanding is achieved. In a real definition it is not the identical wording that is sought; only the person preoccupied with descriptive formulations circles endlessly around expressions, directing all his objections and fascination at them.","note":"The Arabic contains ḥāssat al-ʿayn, and the example concerns contextual disambiguation of a homonym."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"The fourth rule: how to obtain a definition. Understand that a definition is not obtained by demonstration. Suppose we say by way of defining wine, “It is an intoxicating beverage,” and someone asks “Why?” We cannot properly establish that definition by demonstration. Even if we are not arguing with an opponent but seeking the definition for ourselves, how could we find it by demonstration? The sentence “Wine is an intoxicating beverage” is a proposition whose subject is wine and whose predicate is “intoxicating beverage.” If that proposition is known without a middle term, demonstration is unnecessary. If it is not known and requires a middle term—which is what demonstration means, namely, seeking the middle—then the fitness of the middle term for the subject and the fitness of the predicate for the middle are each further propositions. By what means are we to know that those propositions are true?","note":"The “middle” is the middle term of syllogistic inference (al-wasaṭ), not the medium of revelation or a legal intermediary."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"If each of them also requires a middle term, the process regresses without limit. If at some point we stop without a middle, how is the truth established at that stopping point? We should use the same route at the outset. For example, suppose we define knowledge as “recognition,” and when challenged, answer, “Because all knowledge is conviction, and every conviction is recognition, it follows that all knowledge is recognition”—a demonstrative form to be discussed later. The reply would be: “Why did you say that all knowledge is conviction, and why did you say that every conviction is recognition?” The original question has now become two questions, and so the regress proceeds without end.","note":"This critique concerns how one *acquires* an essential definition; it does not deny the usefulness of syllogisms in proving propositions once terms are known."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"Rather, when the disagreement is with an opponent, one may say that the definition's correctness has been established by its applying to every appropriate instance and to no inappropriate instance—its inclusion and exclusion (iṭṭirād wa-inʿikās). The opponent must acknowledge that much. He may, however, dispute whether it expresses the whole essence. If he denies that the definition is coextensive even according to his own principles, ask him to state his own definition, compare the two, and identify the added or missing qualification. Isolate the attribute that accounts for the difference, and then establish or refute it by an appropriate method. For instance, we may say: “A usurped thing entails liability; the offspring of a usurped thing is itself usurped; hence liability attaches to it.” The opponent replies: “We do not concede that the offspring of a usurped thing is itself usurped.” We answer: “Usurpation is the assertion of an unlawful controlling hand over another's property.”","note":"The legal case tests the extension of a *definition* and whether offspring fall under it; it is not offered as a conclusive doctrinal ruling on all such cases."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"This kind of controlling hand is indeed present. The opponent may object that it is neither “unlawful” nor properly an “assertion,” maintaining instead that it is only a continued holding. But that is not our present concern. He may concede that the described situation occurs in the offspring, but still deny that it defines usurpation. No demonstration can be offered to prove the definition as such. We can only say: “It includes all and only the right cases. What is your own definition?” He must state it, so that we can identify the point of difference. He then says, “Usurpation is the assertion of a controlling hand that invalidates and removes a rightful hand.” We reply: “You have added the qualification of removal.”","note":"The discussion is dialectical and method-focused; iṭṭirād and inʿikās are adequacy tests rather than a full demonstration of essence."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"We should then see whether we can induce our opponent to acknowledge a case of usurpation in which this added feature is absent. If we can, the additional qualification proves dispensable. We may say: “A usurper…”","note":"Arabic continues this forensic example on the next page."}],"V01P015":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…is liable to the owner for the usurpation, even when he asserts an unlawful controlling hand without actually removing the rightful one, since that hand was already absent.” This is how to resolve a dispute with an interlocutor. As for the person investigating the question on his own, once the essence has become clear to him and he has found an expression that accurately conveys his own determination, he knows that he possesses a definition; there is no point in obstinately arguing against himself.","note":"Continuation of the previous page’s case about usurpation and juridical definition."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"The fifth rule: the principal sources of defect in definitions.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"They are three. A defect may arise from the genus, from the differentia, or from something shared between them. A defect in the genus occurs when one substitutes the differentia for the genus, as in defining passionate love as “excess of love.” One should instead say “love carried to excess,” since excess distinguishes it from the other kinds of love. Another error is to substitute the material substrate for the genus. Thus one might define a chair as “wood on which one sits” or a sword as “iron with which one cuts.” One should instead define the sword as “a manufactured instrument of iron, elongated to such and such a breadth and used for cutting in such and such a manner.” “Instrument” supplies the genus; iron is the matter in which its form is realized, not the genus.","note":"Important distinction between conceptual genus and underlying material; “matter” (maḥall) here is not a legal locus of obligation."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"Even more remote from a proper definition is substituting something which once existed but no longer exists: defining ash as “burned wood,” or a child as “transformed seminal fluid.” Iron still exists in the sword, whereas the seminal fluid no longer exists as such in the child, nor wood in the ash.","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"Another mistake is to put a part in place of the genus, as when one defines ten as “five and five.” One may also substitute the capacity for doing something in place of the thing actually done. Thus “a chaste person is one capable of abstaining from sensual pleasures” is defective; the chaste person is the one who actually refrains, since the immoral person may possess the ability to refrain while not doing so. Another mistake is to substitute an inessential inseparable concomitant for the genus, as if one made “one” or “existent” the genus in defining the sun or the earth.","note":"The distinction of act from power recurs in later moral and juridical discussions, but this passage itself concerns the construction of definitions."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"Another error is to put species in the place of genus, as when one says “evil is injustice toward other people,” though injustice is only one species of evil. Defects in the differentia occur when one uses inseparable or incidental properties instead of essential constituents to distinguish the thing, or fails to mention all its differentiae. Shared defects include defining something by what is more obscure than it—for example, defining an originated thing as “what power relates to.”","note":""},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"Another shared defect is defining something through what is equally obscure, as in “knowledge is that by which one knows” or “that by which a subject is knowing.” Another is defining a contrary through its contrary, as when someone says “knowledge is what is neither conjecture nor ignorance,” and proceeds to exclude each opposing state; or “an even number is one that is not odd.” One could then define the odd as “what is not even,” producing a circle that explains nothing. Yet another is including one correlate in the definition of its reciprocal when the two are on an equal footing. For example: “A father is one who has a son,” followed by “A son is one who has a father.” One should instead say: “A father is an animal from whose reproductive substance another animal of the same species has come to be”; he is a father in virtue of this relation, without making his definition refer back to the son, since their respective intelligibility or obscurity goes together. Similarly, someone defines the sun as “the celestial body that rises by day.” Asked what day is, he must then answer, if he wishes to be correct, “the time from the sun's rising to its setting.” There are countless similar cases.","note":"The objection concerns explanatory circularity, not logical contradiction as such. Do not map the example of biological fatherhood onto the full juristic concept of nasab."},{"sourceParagraph":7,"en":"The sixth rule: that a meaning which admits of no composition at all cannot be defined except by way of…","note":"Incomplete sentence at digital page break; the sixth rule continues on V01P016 and requires continuation before the section can be marked finished."}],"V01P016":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…by explaining the expression, or by means of a descriptive account (rasm), but not by a real definition (ḥadd ḥaqīqī). An instance of a simple meaning is “existent” (mawjūd). If someone asks you to define “existent,” the most you can say is “thing” (shayʾ), or “what is established” (thābit). You have merely replaced one name by a synonym, which may communicate no more than the first. One expression may even be less familiar in a given language: “What is ʿaqār?” “Wine.” “What is ghaḍanfar?” “Lion.”"},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"Even this response is useful only if the answer is more familiar than the word in the question, and it remains only a verbal explanation. Someone seeking an account of a lion’s essence cannot obtain it by hearing another name for it: one must say, for example, “a predatory animal of such-and-such characteristics.” Synonyms do not suffice. If you define the existent as “what is known” or “what is mentioned,” and add a qualification to exclude the nonexistent, you have mentioned a concomitant rather than its essence; the result is descriptive, not real. Existence, then, admits of no real definition: since it is the starting point of every explanation, how could it itself be explained? We say a simple meaning has no real definition because asking for a thing’s ḥadd is rather like asking for the boundaries of a house. Those boundaries are the distinct directions in which the house ends, and one delimits it by stating the different enclosing limits around it."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"Asking “What is the definition of blackness?” amounts to requesting the meanings and realities whose combination constitutes blackness. Blackness is a colour, an existent, an accident (ʿaraḍ), a visible, knowable, and mentionable thing; it may be one or many, bright, lustrous, dull, and have other attributes. Some attributes are accidents which can disappear; some are inseparable concomitants, but not essentials—such as being knowable or being called one or many. Some are essential, so that blackness cannot be understood without their being understood, as in its being a colour."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The person seeking a definition asks, as it were, of how many meanings the reality of blackness consists. The different constituents must be assembled, proceeding from the more general to the more particular, without including accidents; indeed one may demand essentials alone, omitting even inseparable concomitants. If a meaning, such as existence, is not assembled from several essentials, how could it be defined? It would be like asking for the boundary of a sphere while imagining that the whole world is a single sphere. It cannot be bounded like a house. Its limit is its outer surface, a single uniform surface, not distinct surfaces ending at different places."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"Although this sensible analogy is remote from the subject, it may convey my intention. When I say blackness is made up conceptually of colourhood and blackness, colourhood being the genus and blackness the species, do not suppose that blackness contains several really distinct and unequal entities. Do not say that blackness is both a colour and another thing called blackness: this very colour is black. Its content is composite or multiple for the intellect, which can understand colourhood generally without conceiving blackness, then understand blackness as something further. Those are undeniable distinctions within thought, but not separate entities in external existence. Nor imagine that someone who denies the theological doctrine of “states” (aḥwāl) can really define anything."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"The theologians call colourhood a “state” (ḥāl). For if the denier of states gives only the genus, his definition is defective. If he adds a distinguishing qualification, ask whether this addition is the very same as the first meaning or something different. If identical, it is repetition and should be discarded. If different, he has acknowledged two conceptually distinguishable elements."},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"If he defines substance (jawhar) merely as “existent,” the definition also covers accidents and fails. If he adds that it occupies a spatial position (mutaḥayyiz), ask: is the meaning of “spatially positioned” identical with or different from that of “existent”? If identical, it is as though he has said…"}],"V01P017":[{"sourceParagraph":0,"en":"…“existent, existent.” Synonyms are equivalent to repetition, so the definition again fails to exclude accidents. But if the meaning differs—if “spatially positioned” blocks the objection that “existent” did not—then it is a difference in meaning, not merely wording. A distinction between the two intelligible meanings must be acknowledged."},{"sourceParagraph":1,"en":"The purpose of this argument is to show that what is simple cannot have a real definition. It may have a verbal definition—“existent” means “thing”—or a descriptive definition, such as saying that existents comprise creator and created, powerful and acted-upon, one and many, eternal and originated, enduring and perishing, or any other inseparable concomitants. None tells us what existence itself is; each indicates something that invariably accompanies it. Know, too, that when a composite is defined through its essential constituents, each constituent may itself be subjected to the demand for definition. Asked what a tree is, you say “a plant standing upon a trunk”; asked what a plant is, “a growing body”; asked what a body is, “a composite substance,” or “a substance having length, breadth, and depth”; asked what substance is, and so on. Every composite consists of elements whose realities may themselves be analysed."},{"sourceParagraph":2,"en":"Do not suppose this goes on indefinitely. It ultimately reaches simple meanings apprehended immediately by intellect or sense, which do not require a request for definition. The same applies to assertoric knowledge (ʿulūm taṣdīqiyya): propositions may require demonstration, a demonstration consists of two premises, and either premise might call for a further demonstration, until the sequence reaches primary propositions. As there are first principles in assertoric knowledge, so there are primary conceptions. The person seeking definitions of the primaries asks for explanations of words, not of their realities, since these are already present in the intellect through its original disposition—just as it grasps existence itself. Demanding their reality anew is obstinate, like demanding proof that two exceeds one. This concludes the rules we intended to give."},{"sourceParagraph":3,"en":"The second part of the discussion of definition: exercises applying the rules to particular definitions."},{"sourceParagraph":4,"en":"We have supplied many examples in Miʿyār al-ʿIlm and Miḥakk al-Naẓar. Here we shall restrict ourselves to the definition of “definition,” of “knowledge,” and of “obligation,” because this kind of discussion is ancillary to legal theory and does not warrant an exhaustive treatment here."},{"sourceParagraph":5,"en":"First exercise. People have disagreed about the definition of “definition.” One says that a thing’s definition is its reality and essence. Another says that it is an expression explicating its meaning in a way that both includes everything that belongs to it and excludes everything else. A third pronounces this a disputed issue and argues for one against the other. Observe how confused that third person is: disagreement is possible only when the parties address the same thing. The first two explanations concern distinct things and do not meet upon a common object. The error arises from failing to recognize an equivocal name (ism mushtarak). One who defines ʿayn as the organ which perceives colours through sight does not contradict one who defines ʿayn as a mineral substance prized as currency. The two have defined different things sharing the same expression. Understand this principle: it has extensive uses. If you ask which definition of “definition” I accept, know that seeking meanings by starting from words is to go astray—like turning away from the west while looking for it. Whoever first fixes meanings in the intellect and then gives them appropriate words follows the right course. Let us establish the meanings first. A thing has four levels of being:"},{"sourceParagraph":6,"en":"First: its reality in itself."}]},"technical":[{"term":"taṣawwur","ar":"تصور","preferred":"conception / grasp of an individual meaning","avoid":"mere imagination","why":"It supplies the individual conceptual content prior to propositionally assessable assent.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P009–010; Miʿyār and Miḥakk cited in V01P009"},{"term":"taṣdīq","ar":"تصديق","preferred":"assent to a proposition / assertoric assent","avoid":"simple belief without structure","why":"Negation or affirmation between two conceived terms creates the truth-apt proposition.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P009–010"},{"term":"ḥadd lafẓī","ar":"حد لفظي","preferred":"nominal definition","avoid":"literal definition","why":"It explains the linguistic expression by a known synonym rather than defining the thing.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P010–011"},{"term":"ḥadd rasmī","ar":"حد رسمي","preferred":"descriptive definition","avoid":"official limit","why":"Distinguishes its object by a set of attributes without recovering all essential constituents.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P010–011"},{"term":"ḥadd ḥaqīqī","ar":"حد حقيقي","preferred":"real / essential definition","avoid":"realistic definition","why":"States constitutive essentials of the quiddity; distinct from the fact that the thing exists.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P011–012"},{"term":"māhiyya","ar":"ماهية","preferred":"quiddity (whatness)","avoid":"physical material","why":"The answer to mā huwa, asking what the thing is essentially.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P010–012"},{"term":"dhātī","ar":"ذاتي","preferred":"essential constituent","avoid":"personal","why":"Constitutive of what the thing is, not merely inseparable from it.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P011–012"},{"term":"lāzim","ar":"لازم","preferred":"inseparable concomitant","avoid":"essential constituent","why":"Can accompany a thing necessarily while remaining conceptually subsequent to its quiddity.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P011–012"},{"term":"ʿāriḍ","ar":"عارض","preferred":"incidental attribute","avoid":"accident without qualification","why":"May depart, or can be conceived as absent, without loss of quiddity. Not every occurrence equals Aristotelian accidental substance.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P011–012"},{"term":"jins / nawʿ","ar":"جنس / نوع","preferred":"logical genus / logical species","avoid":"modern biological taxonomic species","why":"Relative places in the Porphyrian ordering described in the text.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P012"},{"term":"burhān","ar":"برهان","preferred":"demonstration; conclusive proof when epistemically warranted","avoid":"any juridical argument","why":"A formally valid inference does not yield certainty unless the premises are certain; compare Opwis’s analysis of Ghazālī’s qiyās.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, OpenITI V01P009–010; Opwis, “Syllogistic Logic in Islamic Legal Theory”"},{"term":"ʿilla","ar":"علة","preferred":"explanatory cause (logic); operative ground (legal analogy), by context","avoid":"universal physical efficient cause","why":"The same term serves different functions in the first support on definition and in legal analysis.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā, V01P011 vs V01P007; Hallaq and Opwis"},{"term":"ḥāl / aḥwāl","ar":"حال / أحوال","preferred":"(theological) state / states","avoid":"psychological condition without qualification","why":"Used in a kalām controversy over intelligibly distinguishable attributes and ontological distinctness.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā V01P016–017"},{"term":"mawjūd","ar":"موجود","preferred":"existent; that which exists","avoid":"a complex genus-and-differentia definition","why":"For al-Ghazālī here, the existent is a primary simple concept lacking a real definition.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā V01P016–017"},{"term":"ism mushtarak","ar":"اسم مشترك","preferred":"equivocal expression; shared name","avoid":"synonym","why":"Different realities may share a word without generating disagreement about a single reality.","source":"al-Mustaṣfā V01P017"}],"note":"Translation of the complete Arabic paragraph in each indexed unit. Page boundaries sometimes interrupt sentences: ellipses in English mark their continuation. Technical renderings remain provisional until editorial collation.","worklinks":[{"work":"Miḥakk al-Naẓar","status":"explicitly named","arabicPage":"V01P009","claim":"Ghazālī directly cites his shorter treatment of definition and demonstration."},{"work":"Miʿyār al-ʿIlm","status":"explicitly named","arabicPage":"V01P009;V01P012","claim":"He cites Miʿyār in the logical introduction and again about the essential/inseparable distinction."},{"work":"Miʿyār al-ʿIlm and Miḥakk al-Naẓar","status":"explicitly named","arabicPage":"V01P017","claim":"Al-Ghazālī explicitly cites these works for fuller exercises in constructing and assessing definitions."}],"status":"The complete Arabic witness is available separately. The opening sequence V00P000 and V01P003–017 now has 105 paragraph-aligned English units; subsequent pages remain untranslated.","coverage_description":"Working source-order translation: V00P000 and V01P003–017 (16 digital page units, 105 aligned paragraph units). Not a completed English translation."};